from pathlib import Path
import numpy as np,json
import matplotlib
matplotlib.use('Agg')
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
O=Path(__file__).parent;f,axs=plt.subplots(2,3,figsize=(13,7),sharex=True)
for col,(day,start) in enumerate([('2026-01-03',447),('2026-01-03',450),('2026-02-15',402)]):
 for suffix,label in [('force-initialized-offset-body','Fixed initial angle'),('causal-alpha-body','Causal angle inference + refit')]:
  a=np.load(O/f'{day}-{start}-{suffix}.npz');pred=a['model'];truth=a['truth'];t=a['t'];yaw=np.unwrap(pred[:,2]);yaw-=2*np.pi*np.round((yaw[0]-truth[0,2])/(2*np.pi));axs[0,col].plot(t,np.rad2deg(pred[:,0]),label=label);axs[1,col].plot(t,np.rad2deg(yaw-truth[0,2]),label=label)
 axs[0,col].plot(t,np.rad2deg(truth[:,0]),'k',lw=2,label='Recorded');axs[1,col].plot(t,np.rad2deg(truth[:,2]-truth[0,2]),'k',lw=2,label='Recorded');axs[0,col].set(title=f'{day}, {start} s',ylabel='Payload bank (deg)');axs[1,col].set(xlabel='Forecast time (s)',ylabel='Heading change (deg)')
 for ax in axs[:,col]:ax.grid(alpha=.2)
axs[0,0].legend(fontsize=8);f.suptitle('Better initial force consistency helps one turn, but does not validate the model');f.tight_layout();f.savefig(O/'iteration-causal-canopy-state.png',dpi=160)
p=O/'physics-development.html';s=p.read_text(encoding='utf-8-sig');s=s.replace('</html>','''<section id="force-initialized-canopy"><h2>Initializing canopy attitude from the measured force</h2><p>The two-body model now estimates canopy orientation from the net force direction, instead of fitting an independent hidden starting attitude against future trajectory data. A quadratic fit to the preceding 0.4 seconds supplies a current specific-force estimate. Subtracting the assumed pusher thrust and projecting normal to inferred air velocity supplies an approximate lift direction. Payload acceleration approximates whole-system acceleration, and payload airspeed approximates canopy airspeed during this orientation construction; both are limitations.</p><pre>F_est = m f_specific − T e_thrust
L_est = F_est − (F_est · e_air) e_air
e_down = −L_est / |L_est|; e_span = e_down × e_air
e_x = cos(α) e_air − sin(α) e_down
e_z = sin(α) e_air + cos(α) e_down</pre><p>Canopy angular velocity comes from successive past-only orientation estimates. The suspension constraint then supplies compatible canopy position and velocity. With this initialization, all six development replays are numerically converged: halving the step from 10 to 5 ms changes attitude by less than 0.0003 degrees. An added-inertia variant drives its provisional tensor scale nearly to zero and offers no improvement; this does not establish negligible physical apparent inertia.</p><p>Allowing asymmetric engagement offsets improves several turns. For example, the Hood River 508-second bank error falls from 8.66 to 2.33 degrees RMS, and late Trout Lake from 8.90 to 4.34 degrees. However, fixed initial angle of attack creates a separate problem: modeled initial acceleration can be more than 1 g away from the measurement.</p><figure><img src="iteration-initial-canopy-force.png" alt="Initial acceleration error before and after inferring canopy angle from current force"><figcaption>Parameters are held fixed for this diagnostic. The inferred angle minimizes current acceleration mismatch under the two-body equations, using no future trajectory. One case reaches the permitted upper angle; inferred angles are model-dependent estimates, not measured canopy angles.</figcaption></figure><p>The next fit repeats that causal angle inference for every candidate parameter set. The canopy angle is constrained to 0.02–0.45 rad on a chosen lift-curve branch. The objective includes the remaining initial force mismatch as well as the subsequent trajectory. This removes the forced common 20-degree initial angle and its large artificial transient.</p><figure><img src="iteration-causal-canopy-state.png" alt="Measured bank and heading compared with fixed-angle and causally inferred canopy-state models"><figcaption>These are three-second free predictions after initialization. The refit improves the first Hood River turn but still misses the continued tight turn and the early Trout Lake turn. They are development cases, not untouched validation data.</figcaption></figure><p>After refitting, the 447-second Hood River bank error improves to 5.17 degrees RMS with final heading error −15.8 degrees. At 450 seconds, bank error remains 13.81 degrees and final heading error −84.1 degrees; at Trout Lake 402 seconds, the corresponding errors are 12.08 and +67.3 degrees. The model is still rejected for controller design. More consistent initial force is a worthwhile correction, but it does not repair the sustained-turn dynamics. Pitch stiffness and lateral coefficients also remain weakly constrained or near bounds.</p><p>Evidence: <code>force-initialized-two-body-fit.json</code>, <code>force-initialized-added-body-fit.json</code>, <code>force-initialized-offset-body-fit.json</code>, <code>force-inferred-alpha.json</code>, <code>causal-alpha-body-fit.json</code>. Reproduction: <code>fit_force_initialized_two_body.py</code>, <code>infer_force_initial_alpha.py</code>, <code>fit_causal_alpha_body.py</code>. Geometry and inertia remain provisional; the exact per-flight PWM-to-line-travel calibration is retained.</p></section></html>''');p.write_text(s,encoding='utf-8')
